  ngrep (uses ripgrep internally)
    ngrep <string> 
        Search notes for a string inside the folder that 'current' points to.

  nlist (list and manage notes) [Python version]
    nlist
        List all Markdown notes in the current folder with:
        • File numbers (for easy reference)
        • Modification timestamps (YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS)
        • Note titles (extracted from "# Title:" line if present)
        • Subfolder list with file counts
        Output is saved to $NOTES_RESULTS_FILE and displayed.

    nlist -delete <num>
        Move the Nth note to /tmp/notes/delete (soft delete).
        Example: nlist -delete 3
        Note: Files are not permanently deleted, can be recovered from /tmp/notes/delete

    nlist -rename <num> <new_name>
        Rename the Nth note to <new_name>.
        The .md extension is added automatically if omitted.
        Example: nlist -rename 2 meeting-notes
                 → Renames note #2 to "meeting-notes.md"

    nlist -rename <num> -style date
        Prepend today's date (YYYY-MM-DD-) to the Nth note's filename.
        If the note already has a date prefix, it will be replaced with today's date.
        Example: nlist -rename 1 -style date
                 → "my-note.md" becomes "2025-11-22-my-note.md"
                 → "2025-01-01-old.md" becomes "2025-11-22-old.md"

    nlist -move <num> <folder>
        Move the Nth note into a subfolder.
        • Supports full folder names: nlist -move 5 archive
        • Supports partial matching: nlist -move 5 arc (matches "archive")
        • Use ".." to move to parent directory: nlist -move 2 ..
        Example: nlist -move 3 research
                 → Moves note #3 into the "research" subfolder

    nlist -tags
        List all tags found in notes with file counts.
        Example output:
          #food                          (12 files)
          #recipe                        (8 files)
          #work                          (25 files)

    nlist -tag <tags>
        Filter notes by tags (comma-separated for OR logic).
        Example: nlist -tag food        → Show notes with #food
                 nlist -tag food,recipe → Show notes with #food OR #recipe

    nlist --help
        Show detailed help and usage examples.

    Tips:
    • Run 'nlist' first to see numbered list, then use the number
    • Numbers change after operations, so re-run nlist to get updated numbers
    • The bash version is still available as 'nlist-bash' if needed

  nnote (create a new note)
    nnote <filename.md>
        Create a new Markdown file in the current folder.

  nresults (show search results)
    nresults
        Print the saved results.txt file (file index + names from last search).

  nopen (open a note by index)
    nopen
        Open the first file in results.txt.
    nopen <index>
        Open the file at the specified index from results.txt.

  nsearch (search notes and save results)
    nsearch <string>
        Search notes for a string, save matching filenames to results.txt.

  ncurrent (manage and navigate folders)
    ncurrent
        List subfolders and show the currently selected folder.
    ncurrent <index>
        Set the current folder by selecting its index.
    ncurrent -pwd
        Show the full path of the current folder.
    ncurrent -cd <subfolder>
        Change into a subfolder by name.
    ncurrent -up / ..
        Move up one folder level.
    ncurrent -new <folder>
        Create a new subfolder inside the current folder.
    ncurrent -move <i> -to <j>
        Move folder #i into folder #j.
    ncurrent -move <i> -to up
        Move folder #i up one level.
    ncurrent -rename <i> <new_name>
        Rename folder #i.
    ncurrent -help
        Show help information.

  nfzf (fuzzy find folders)
    nfzf
        Search subfolders using fzf and open a file in Neovim.

  nrandom (open random note)
    nrandom
        Open a random note with substantial content (≥10 lines).
        Great for:
        • Rediscovering old notes
        • Daily review/reflection
        • Serendipitous connections
        Shows which note is being opened before launching nvim.

  nlist2 (interactive note selector)
    nlist2
        Interactive curses-based note selector with preview pane.
        Features:
        • Vim-style navigation (j/k, g/G)
        • Live preview of note content
        • Delete functionality with visual feedback

    nlist2 -tags
        List all tags with counts (same as nlist -tags).

    nlist2 -tag <tags>
        Interactive selector filtered by tags.
        Example: nlist2 -tag food,recipe → Only show notes with #food OR #recipe

    Interactive Keys:
        j/k         Navigate down/up
        g/G         Jump to top/bottom
        d           Toggle delete mark (highlighted in red)
        Enter       Open selected note (or delete marked notes if any)
        q/Esc       Quit

    Tips:
    • Use nlist2 when you want to preview notes before opening
    • Press 'd' on multiple notes to batch delete
    • Marked notes stay highlighted until you press Enter or unmark with 'd'

  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  LINKING AND BACKLINKS
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Link Format
    Notes use wiki-style double-bracket links with 8-character hex IDs:
        [[14ba882b|Title of Note]]  ← Link with title
        [[14ba882b]]                ← Link with ID only

    Every note automatically gets a unique ID when created or saved:
        <!-- id: 14ba882b -->
    (This appears at the top of the file as an HTML comment)

  Creating Links (in Neovim)
    Keybinding: <leader>ln

    Interactive fuzzy search to find and insert a link:
    1. Press <leader>ln
    2. Choose search method:
       • (c)ontent  - Search file contents (default)
       • (f)ilenames - Search by filename
       • (t)itles - Search by note titles
    3. Type to filter, select with Enter
    4. Link is inserted: [[ID|Title]]

    Example Workflow:
    - You're writing: "I learned about cooking today, see..."
    - Press <leader>ln
    - Search for "pasta"
    - Select "My Pasta Recipe" note
    - Result: "...see [[a4f3e2b1|My Pasta Recipe]]"

  Jumping to Linked Notes (in Neovim)
    Keybinding: <leader>gf

    Behavior:
    • Cursor on link: Jump directly to that note
    • Not on link: Show menu of all outgoing links in current note
    • Single link in note: Auto-jump to it

    Example:
    - Cursor on [[14ba882b|Cooking Tips]]
    - Press <leader>gf
    - Opens the Cooking Tips note

  Backlinks (Automatic)
    Backlinks show you which notes link TO the current note.

    Run sync-map.py to generate backlinks:
        python3 sync-map.py

    This scans all notes and adds a "#### Backlinks:" section at the bottom:

        #### Backlinks:
         - [[971109ef|Daily Journal 2025-04-06]]
         - [[5c3174a5|Recipe Collection]]

    Options:
        python3 sync-map.py --strip-only    Remove all backlink sections
        python3 sync-map.py --map-only      Rebuild ID map only
        python3 sync-map.py --inject-only   Inject backlinks (use existing map)
        python3 sync-map.py --dry-run       Show what would change

    The script:
    • Creates .note_map.json (ID → path/title mapping)
    • Logs to .note_map.log
    • Makes backups before modifying files
    • Reports broken links

  Creating a Linked Note Workflow
    Scenario: You're in note A, think of related topic B

    Method 1 (Manual):
    1. In note A, add a link placeholder: [[|New Topic]]
    2. Press <leader>ln to search for the new note
    3. If it doesn't exist, create it with: nnote new-topic.md
    4. Go back to note A, press <leader>ln and link to new-topic.md
    5. Run sync-map.py to generate backlink in new-topic.md

    Method 2 (Using existing note creation):
    1. Create the new note: nnote related-topic.md
    2. In the new note, press <leader>ln to link back to original
    3. Go back to original note, press <leader>ln to link forward
    4. Run sync-map.py to sync all backlinks

    After running sync-map.py, the new note will show:
        #### Backlinks:
         - [[<original-id>|Original Note Title]]

  Tags in Notes
    Add hashtags anywhere in your notes:
        #food #recipe #italian

    Headings (with space) are NOT tags:
        # My Recipe          ← This is a heading, not a tag
        #recipe             ← This IS a tag

    Search by tags:
        nlist -tags              List all tags
        nlist -tag food          Show notes with #food
        nlist -tag food,recipe   Show notes with #food OR #recipe
        nlist2 -tag food         Interactive selector for #food notes

    Tags are cached in .note_tags_cache.json and auto-update when files change.

  Tips for Linking
    • Use <leader>ln liberally - linking is fast and helps connect ideas
    • Run sync-map.py periodically to keep backlinks fresh
    • Jump through notes with <leader>gf for quick exploration
    • Use tags (#) for categories, links ([[ID]]) for relationships
    • Check .note_map.log if you encounter linking issues

  nweb (web-based graph viewer)
    nweb
        Start a web server to visualize your notes as an interactive graph.
        Similar to Obsidian's graph view.

        Features:
        • Interactive graph visualization of note relationships
        • Text search across note titles and content
        • Tag-based filtering (select multiple tags)
        • Click nodes to preview note content
        • Double-click nodes for file path (to open in nvim)
        • Real-time filtering as you search/select tags

        Usage:
        1. Run: nweb
        2. Open browser: http://localhost:8080
        3. Use search bar to find notes
        4. Click tags to filter graph
        5. Press Ctrl+C to stop server

        Port already in use?
        If you see "Address already in use", kill the existing process:
            lsof -ti:8080 | xargs kill

        Tips:
        • Hover over nodes to see titles and tags
        • Select multiple tags for broader filtering (OR logic)
        • Search and tag filters work together
        • Use navigation buttons in graph for zoom/pan


